{
  "skillId": "rationalise-denominator",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "**Rationalising the denominator** means rewriting a fraction so that no surd is left underneath. Because $\\sqrt{b}\\times\\sqrt{b}=b$, multiplying the top and the bottom by the denominator's surd clears the root without changing the value of the fraction — multiplying by $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{b}}{\\sqrt{b}}$ is multiplying by $1$.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt{b}}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{b}}{\\sqrt{b}}=\\dfrac{a\\sqrt{b}}{b}$.",
      "Only the **surd** in the denominator needs clearing: for $\\dfrac{a}{c\\sqrt{d}}$ multiply by $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{d}}{\\sqrt{d}}$, since $c$ is already rational, giving $\\dfrac{a\\sqrt{d}}{cd}$.",
      "For $\\dfrac{a\\sqrt{b}}{c\\sqrt{d}}$ the same move gives $\\dfrac{a\\sqrt{bd}}{cd}$.",
      "A root over a fraction is rewritten first: $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{y}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$, then rationalised.",
      "Finish by simplifying the surd and cancelling any common factor: $\\dfrac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3}=2\\sqrt{3}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Identify the surd in the denominator",
      "Multiply the numerator and denominator by that surd",
      "Simplify the fraction"
    ]
  },
